Comparison of Algorithms:


1. Naive String-Matching Algorithm:

Time Complexity: O((n - m + 1) * m), where n is the length of the text and m is the length of the pattern.
Space Complexity: O(1).
Best for: Small inputs, simple implementation.
Limitations: Inefficient for large inputs due to excessive comparisons.

2. Rabin-Karp Algorithm:

Time Complexity: O(n + m) on average, but O(n * m) in the worst case (when hash collisions occur frequently).
Space Complexity: O(m).
Best for: Searching multiple patterns (can be extended for multiple pattern matching).
Limitations: Hash collisions may reduce performance in some cases.

3. KMP Algorithm:

Time Complexity: O(n + m), where n is the length of the text and m is the length of the pattern.
Space Complexity: O(m) for storing the LPS array.
Best for: Efficient searching for a single pattern.
Limitations: Slightly more complex to implement than Naive or Rabin-Karp.


Performance Comparison:

Naive: Simple but inefficient, especially for large texts and patterns.
Rabin-Karp: Fast in the average case but can degrade due to hash collisions.
KMP: The most efficient in terms of time complexity for single-pattern matching.

How to Test Performance:
When running the code, enter a text string and a pattern string, and the algorithm will print the matched indices along with the execution time in nanoseconds for comparison.
